Spec: auth

Purpose:
  Turn one request's wire facts into the @Identity — a `user` string plus `groups` — that
    ./authz.rs copies verbatim into a @SubjectAccessReview, via exactly two paths: the
    front-proxy `X-Remote-User`/`X-Remote-Group` headers honoured only while the
    `trust_request_header` flag is set, and an `Authorization: Bearer` token validated by a
    cluster @TokenReview call; every failure is the unit @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired.
    The file itself never inspects transport, TLS or certificates: the mandatory-mTLS
    guarantee behind the header trust lies in @server::server::run_server, owned by
    ./server.rs.

Owns:
  ./auth.rs

References:
  ./error.sdd — the @DiagError vocabulary, and the `DIAG-006` / `401` mapping plus
    @IntoResponse rendering of every failure this file raises.
  ./config.sdd — the `trust_request_header` option and its `--trust-request-header` /
    `DIAG_TRUST_REQUEST_HEADER` spellings; this file only consumes the resolved @bool.
  ./server.rs — spec pending: the startup guardrail refusing the trust flag together with
    `insecure_no_tls`, the client-cert-verifying mTLS built by @build_mtls_config, the
    @instance_handler call site and the @RequestHeaders extractor that feeds this module.
  ./authz.rs — spec pending: sole in-crate reader of @Identity.user and @Identity.groups.
  /server/src/error.sdd, /common/src/context.sdd — approved calibration references.

Must:
  @request_header_identity is a pure parse with no trust decision, no cluster call and no
    transport check: the first `X-Remote-User` occurrence must @to_str cleanly (visible
    ASCII) and be non-empty else the result is @None even when groups are valid, and its
    value is kept verbatim — no trim, no case fold; groups are the @get_all of every
    `X-Remote-Group` occurrence in arrival order, each that @to_str cleanly, repeats and
    empty strings kept, values with non-visible bytes skipped silently without failing the
    parse, none at all yielding an empty @Vec. @HeaderMap lookup matches header names
    case-insensitively per HTTP; `X-Remote-User` and `X-Remote-Group` are the canonical
    front-proxy spellings and the only two names read.
  @extract_identity consults @request_header_identity only when the `trust_request_header`
    argument is literally `true`, and a @Some short-circuits: @AUTHORIZATION is never read,
    no cluster call happens, and a `Bearer` token riding along is never examined. A `true`
    flag whose parse yields @None continues exactly as `false` does; under `false` the
    front-proxy headers are unread however well-formed, so this flag read is the module's
    entire trust decision — no peer-certificate or transport fact is bound to it here (see
    Tasks).
  The Bearer path reads @AUTHORIZATION — first occurrence only — only after the header path
    produced no identity: a value that fails @to_str yields @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired
    with no malformed-vs-absent distinction; an identity comes only from the remainder of
    @strip_prefix against the exact case-sensitive seven-byte prefix `Bearer ` trailing-space
    included, so `bearer <t>`, `BEARER <t>`, `Bearer` without the space and any other scheme
    end in @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired; acceptance logs nothing and echoes nothing.
  @validate_token POSTs exactly one @TokenReview through @api::@Api::all with
    @PostParams::default: @spec.token carries the @token byte-identical — `""` included,
    never shape-checked — and that is the whole spec, `spec.audiences` never set, leaving
    audience acceptance to the cluster's default; @kube::@Client is cloned per call with no
    caching.
  A @TokenReview verdict counts only as literal `true` in `status.authenticated`: identity
    fields `username` and `groups` arrive verbatim or through @unwrap_or_default as `""` and
    an empty @Vec — an `authenticated` reply omitting `username` still yields an accepted
    @Identity with `user` `""` and nothing here rejects it — while a missing @status, or
    @authenticated @None or @false, is refused.
  The file's only two log statements live in @validate_token: @tracing::error! with
    `TokenReview failed: {e}` carrying the raw @kube::@Error @Display when the POST
    itself fails, and @tracing::warn! with `Token authentication failed` when the verdict
    refuses — neither names the token, the presented identity or the caller; a successful
    authentication logs nothing.
  The failure is unitary: the only value both async entries @Err with is
    @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired — header, token, byte-shape, cluster-outage and
    refused-token legs are byte-identical on the wire (`401` `DIAG-006` per ./error.sdd) —
    and a @kube::@Error from the POST is converted, not wrapped, so the @source chain
    survives only in the error log line and no @DiagError::KubeError can come from here.
  Certificate identity is parsed nowhere: no DN, no CN, no client-cert subject read exists
    in this file — a reimplementation must not invent one — because verified apiserver
    client-certificates authenticate the channel in ./server.rs, while callers authenticate
    through header text or @TokenReview user info only.
  Nothing here is an axum extractor: the file defines no @FromRequestParts impl; the
    consumer clones headers through @server::server::@RequestHeaders and awaits
    @extract_identity inside @instance_handler after route-shape validation and before
    authorization, and the failing `?` reaches `401` through the @IntoResponse rendered in
    ./error.sdd — header plumbing and call ordering are the consumer's contract.

Depends on:
  @crate::error::@DiagError and its unit variant @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired —
    ./error.rs as specified in ./error.sdd, the only error type produced here.
  @kube::@Client, @kube::@Api and @api::@PostParams — kube `3.1.0` from /Cargo.toml; the
    @create POST for @TokenReview is the module's only cluster access.
  @k8s_openapi::api::authentication::v1::@TokenReview and @TokenReviewSpec — k8s-openapi
    `0.27.1` from /Cargo.toml, the wire shape of the token path.
  @axum `0.8` — @axum::http::@HeaderMap with @get, @get_all and @to_str, plus the
    @AUTHORIZATION constant from @axum::http::header; the @HeaderValue::to_str visible-ASCII
    rule decides which header bytes can become identity at all.
  @tracing — @tracing::error! and @tracing::warn! for the two log statements in Must.
  @std::string::@ToString and @option::@Option::unwrap_or_default / @bool::@unwrap_or — the
    infallible defaulting that replaces any @unwrap or @expect.

Exposes:
  @Identity — `pub @struct` deriving @Debug and @Clone with two `pub` fields, `user`:
    @std::string::@String and `groups`: @std::vec::@Vec<@std::string::@String>; `pub`
    through `pub mod auth;` in ./lib.rs; reaches ./authz.rs SAR subjects and ./server.rs.
  @extract_identity — `pub async fn(@client: &@kube::@Client, @headers:
    &@axum::http::@HeaderMap, @trust_request_header: @bool) -> @Result<@Identity,
    @DiagError>`: the module's decision point, its only item used outside this file.
  @request_header_identity — @must_use `pub fn(@headers: &@axum::http::@HeaderMap) ->
    @Option<@Identity>`: trust-free parse, kept `pub` though no in-crate caller besides
    @extract_identity and the tests uses it.
  @validate_token — `pub async fn(@client: &@kube::@Client, @token: &@std::primitive::@str)
    -> @Result<@Identity, @DiagError>`: the @TokenReview round trip, likewise `pub` beyond
    its single in-file caller.

Accepts:
  @extract_identity: a borrowed @kube::@Client that only the Bearer path touches — the
    header path performs zero cluster I/O; @axum::http::@HeaderMap as-is with no shape
    precondition; one @bool, the per-request entire trust authority, flowing here from
    `Config.trust_request_header` through @crate::@AppState — the module reads no config.
  @request_header_identity: @axum::http::@HeaderMap alone.
  @validate_token: any @std::primitive::@str left after the `Bearer ` prefix, `""` included,
    posted byte-identical; client reachability is never probed beforehand.

Returns:
  @Ok(@Identity) from @extract_identity: the header identity whenever the flag is `true`
    and `X-Remote-User` is clean visible-ASCII and non-empty, groups as presented in
    arrival order and possibly empty; otherwise the token identity whenever @TokenReview
    authenticated `true` — `user` `""` included when the cluster omitted `username`.
  @request_header_identity: @Some of the verbatim user plus collected groups, or @None when
    `X-Remote-User` is absent, non-visible-ASCII or empty.
  @Err(@DiagError::AuthenticationRequired) for every remaining combination, rendered
    `401` `DIAG-006` by ./error.sdd.
  @Identity itself adds no method and no helper: @Debug is its sole output surface, and no
    identity ever reaches a log from this module.

Raises:
  @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired — the lone failure of @extract_identity and of
    @validate_token, from: headers ignored (flag `false`) or refused (absent, empty or
    non-visible `X-Remote-User`) while the Bearer path is missing an @AUTHORIZATION value,
    any non-`Bearer `-prefixed value, a malformed value, a failed @TokenReview POST, or a
    reply where `status`, `authenticated` or its @true value is missing.
  No leg produces a message identifying itself, and no variant other than
    @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired — @DiagError::InternalError and @DiagError::KubeError
    never originate here even when the cluster is down.
  No panic path exists: @unwrap and @expect appear nowhere outside the `#[cfg(test)]`
    module, every option defaulting is total, and malformed @HeaderMap or @HeaderValue
    input is refused, not fatal.

Handles:
  Repeated `authorization` occurrences — only the first is ever parsed.
  `X-Remote-Group` occurrences with non-visible bytes — that occurrence drops, surviving
    siblings keep their order.
  Trust flag `true` with headers absent or refused — silent fall-through into the Bearer
    path, indistinguishable to the client.
  Co-present `X-Remote-User` and `Bearer` under the flag — the header identity wins outright
    and the token is never validated.
  An empty token after a valid prefix — forwarded verbatim, the cluster holds the decision.
  A @TokenReview reply without @status — refused exactly like an explicit @false verdict.

Tasks:
  [ ] Cover @request_header_identity details the three existing tests leave free: groups
      without a user yielding @None; non-visible bytes in the user value yielding @None; a
      missing `X-Remote-Group` yielding empty @groups; an empty-string group kept; a
      non-visible group dropped while siblings survive.
  [ ] Cover @extract_identity ordering and trust gating: under `false` a valid
      `X-Remote-User` is ignored and a `Bearer` token still decides, and under `true` with
      both sources the token is never validated and zero cluster call happens — every
      @extract_identity test needs a constructible @kube::@Client (e.g. a fake-apiserver
      @kube::@config::@Config) and the crate ships none: ./Cargo.toml @dev-dependencies is
      empty.
  [ ] Cover the Bearer-shape refusals — `Bearer` bare, `bearer <t>`, `Token <t>`, and a
      non-visible-byte @AUTHORIZATION value all landing @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired
      before any cluster call — plus the empty-`Bearer ` suffix reaching @validate_token.
  [ ] Cover @validate_token verdicts against @TokenReview replies — `authenticated` `true`
      with and without `username`/`groups`, `false`, missing @status, and a failing POST —
      asserting the two log texts, blocked on the same missing fake-apiserver harness.
  [?] The trust boundary proper: under `true` the file accepts whatever literal string
      `X-Remote-User` carries with no further check, and safety rests entirely on
      ./server.rs refusing to start the flag insecurely and pinning client-cert
      verification — nothing at @extract_identity binds the parse to that verified channel,
      so reuse over any other listener would silently permit impersonation. Decide later
      whether the extractor itself must prove the mTLS peer or the config-level coupling
      stays.
  [?] A genuine `Bearer` credential co-present with trusted headers is discarded unexamined,
      header presence pre-empting any cluster confirmation of it. Decide later whether the
      header path cross-checks a presented token.
  [?] An @Ok identity may carry `user` `""` when the cluster's `username` is omitted, and
      that empty subject flows on into @SubjectAccessReview. Decide later whether
      an empty `user` should be refused as @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired instead.
  [?] A failing @TokenReview POST — cluster down, RBAC denial on `tokenreviews`, any
    @kube::@Error — lands in the same client-failure lane as a bad token, masking
    server-side misconfiguration at clients and dropping the @source chain outside the log.
      Decide later whether transport failures become @DiagError::KubeError instead.
  [?] The `Bearer ` prefix is byte- and case-exact, refusing `bearer <t>` / `BEARER <t>` /
      spaceless `Bearer` although RFC 7235 scheme tokens are case-insensitive. Decide later
      whether the parse loosens.
  [?] Nothing logs a resolved identity and the refusal warn names no presented string, so
      neither audit trails nor spoofing forensics are possible from this module's output.
      Decide later whether an info/debug line carrying the resolved identity (or an
      anonymized digest) is added.
  [?] @validate_token and @request_header_identity are `pub` with no consumer outside this
      file (in-crate only @extract_identity and @Identity are imported), against the
      project's keep-public-surface-narrow rule. Decide later whether they become
      `pub(crate)`.

Done when:
  Every Scenario below maps to a passing test (the `[ ]` Tasks or their successors), the
    `[?]` decisions above are recorded, and `cargo test -p server` is green.

Scenario: Front-proxy header identity is parsed verbatim
  Given `X-Remote-User: alice` with `X-Remote-Group: g1` then `g2` appended
  When @request_header_identity runs
  Then @Some of the @Identity with `user` `alice` and @groups [`g1`, `g2`] in arrival order
  Maps to test @parses_request_header_identity
  But given no headers, or `X-Remote-User` of `""`, the result is @None
  Maps to test @no_identity_without_headers
  Maps to test @empty_remote_user_is_rejected
  And the only-group, empty-group, dropped-group and details legs above have no mapped test.

Scenario: Untrusted front-proxy headers are ignored
  Given `X-Remote-User: alice`, a group header, and @trust_request_header `false`
  When @extract_identity runs, with or without `Authorization: Bearer tok`
  Then no header identity is ever returned
  And every Bearer-shape refusal ends @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: Trusted headers win over the token
  Given @trust_request_header `true`, clean `X-Remote-User`/`X-Remote-Group` and
    `Authorization: Bearer secret-token`
  When @extract_identity runs
  Then the header identity is returned, @AUTHORIZATION is untouched, zero @TokenReview is
    sent and `secret-token` is never validated nor logged
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: Bearer prefix must match byte for byte
  Given @trust_request_header `false` and @AUTHORIZATION values `Bearer abc`, `bearer abc`,
    `Token abc` and `Bearer`
  When @extract_identity parses each
  Then the first continues into @validate_token with token `abc`
  And the other three end @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired before any cluster call
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: TokenReview verdict shapes the identity
  Given @TokenReview replies whose `status.authenticated` is `true` with `username`
    `system:alice` and @groups [`dev`], `true` with both fields absent, `false`, @None, or a
    missing @status, and one failing POST
  When @validate_token maps each
  Then the verdicts are an @Identity of `system:alice` / [`dev`]; an @Identity with `user`
    `""` and empty @groups; @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired for the last three, the
    refusals carrying the warn `Token authentication failed` and the failing POST the error
    line `TokenReview failed: {e}` with the raw @kube::@Error text
  No mapped test exists.

Example:
  parsed header identity: @Identity fields `user: "alice"`, `groups: ["g1", "g2"]`
  shared wire refusal for every failing leg: `DIAG-006: Authentication required - no valid
    identity provided` with status `401`
  token-path request shape: a @TokenReview whose `spec.token` equals the seven-byte prefix
    stripped remainder of `Authorization`, `spec.audiences` absent
